        {
          "heading": "Concept",
          "content": "Several sources point toward designing quantum protocols around what a physical platform can reliably prepare, transform, and measure. The photonic variational solver proposes a device ansatz: build the device, characterize the states it can prepare within coherence limits, and optimize within that accessible family. The quantum-internet source treats interfaces similarly at the networking layer, emphasizing reversible conversion between matter and light, cavity-QED interactions, and memory mappings that preserve coherence and entanglement."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Cross-source synthesis",
          "content": "The shared concept is hardware-native quantum state engineering. Source 04 applies it to computation by using phase shifters, a CNOT gate, photon pairs, and detectors to prepare and measure two-qubit states for energy estimation. Source 03 applies it to communication by using controlled cavity pulses, dark-state adiabatic passage, and electromagnetically induced transparency to map quantum states between stationary matter systems and flying optical fields."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Implication",
          "content": "This topic is synthesis-worthy because both computation and networking are constrained by physical interfaces, not just abstract algorithms. The evidence supports a page about platform-shaped protocols, while leaving open which hardware-native approaches will scale best."
        }
